WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:03.799
In this part of our optimization tools,

2
00:00:03.799 --> 00:00:05.839
I want to talk about the Merge Actor tool,

3
00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:08.240
as the Merge Actor tool is the basis

4
00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:12.039
for a lot of different optimization workflows inside

5
00:00:12.039 --> 00:00:12.880
of UE5.

6
00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:16.320
Now at its core, what the Merge Actor tool does

7
00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.719
is it takes a group of static meshes

8
00:00:18.719 --> 00:00:21.879
and allows you to merge them so

9
00:00:21.879 --> 00:00:23.359
that we get one static mesh.

10
00:00:23.359 --> 00:00:24.800
Now we can take this even further

11
00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:27.160
and merge the materials as well so

12
00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:30.719
that we get one static mesh and one material.

13
00:00:30.719 --> 00:00:32.799
Now in the screen in front of us, we

14
00:00:32.799 --> 00:00:35.880
have an example of an area where merging

15
00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:37.079
would be really beneficial.

16
00:00:37.079 --> 00:00:40.240
This little area here is comprised of 61 static meshes

17
00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:41.799
and 19 materials.

18
00:00:41.799 --> 00:00:44.280
It's also an area that's outside of the interaction

19
00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.119
area for this experience.

20
00:00:46.119 --> 00:00:49.039
So this is a lot of static meshes, and more importantly,

21
00:00:49.039 --> 00:00:51.799
a lot of materials that are in an area

22
00:00:51.799 --> 00:00:53.619
that the user isn't going to see up close

23
00:00:53.619 --> 00:00:55.320
or interact with in any way.

24
00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.240
So we want to make sure that those areas are

25
00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.960
as cheap as possible to render.

26
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.000
We've done this in the image on the right-hand side

27
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:07.280
by combining all of those meshes into one static mesh

28
00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:08.400
with one material.

29
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.000
Now they both come in at 51k triangles,

30
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:13.359
but that's not really that big of a concern.

31
00:01:13.359 --> 00:01:16.239
The bigger concern is the amount of meshes

32
00:01:16.239 --> 00:01:19.079
and the amount of materials, because the more that we

33
00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:21.400
can reduce the amount of meshes and materials,

34
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.519
the more we can reduce draw calls

35
00:01:23.519 --> 00:01:26.599
or how many items have to be drawn to the screen per frame,

36
00:01:26.599 --> 00:01:29.040
which is going to increase our performance.

37
00:01:29.079 --> 00:01:33.719
Now to activate the Merge Actor tool,

38
00:01:33.719 --> 00:01:35.200
we're going to go up to our tools

39
00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.280
and then select Merge Actors.

40
00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:39.879
And then all of the Merge Actor tool options

41
00:01:39.879 --> 00:01:40.920
will become available.

42
00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:43.379
Let's pop over to the editor now and see how this works.

43
00:01:43.379 --> 00:01:45.719
So again, we're going to come up here to Tools,

44
00:01:45.719 --> 00:01:47.879
and then we're going to go to Merge Actors.

45
00:01:47.879 --> 00:01:50.879
And by default, we have the merge, but if I click on this,

46
00:01:50.879 --> 00:01:52.879
you can see we have Merge, Simplify, Batch,

47
00:01:52.879 --> 00:01:53.799
and Approximate.

48
00:01:53.799 --> 00:01:56.560
So each one of these will merge our actors

49
00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:59.239
in a different way, shape, or form.

50
00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.920
Let's take a look at what merge does by selecting

51
00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:05.040
these chairs and this table.

52
00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:09.159
And we're just going to go with the default settings, which

53
00:02:09.159 --> 00:02:11.879
is we're going to disable Merge Materials.

54
00:02:11.879 --> 00:02:15.280
And we're going to go ahead and just select Merge Actors.

55
00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:17.240
We'll come up to our merged assets,

56
00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:21.039
and we will just overwrite this static mesh one.

57
00:02:21.039 --> 00:02:22.199
And that's already done.

58
00:02:22.199 --> 00:02:23.280
Look how quick that was.

59
00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.599
Let's just inspect this first and see what we've got.

60
00:02:25.599 --> 00:02:28.599
So 11,908 triangles.

61
00:02:28.639 --> 00:02:31.560
That's not the greatest, but we do

62
00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:33.759
have one mesh with four materials on it.

63
00:02:33.759 --> 00:02:37.039
So if we find this in our Content Browser

64
00:02:37.039 --> 00:02:39.080
here and pull it out, there we go.

65
00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:41.960
So that is one mesh with four materials,

66
00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:44.199
still 11,000 triangles.

67
00:02:44.199 --> 00:02:46.240
We could live with that, but let's see what

68
00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:48.360
else we can do with this tool.

69
00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:50.039
So let's reselect these.

70
00:02:50.039 --> 00:02:51.360
And now what we're going to do

71
00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:55.199
is I'm going to enable our merge materials.

72
00:02:55.199 --> 00:02:57.000
This is going to merge everything down

73
00:02:57.039 --> 00:02:58.919
so that we have one mesh, one material.

74
00:02:58.919 --> 00:03:00.199
Let's go ahead and do that now.

75
00:03:00.199 --> 00:03:01.719
So we're going to say Merge Actors.

76
00:03:01.719 --> 00:03:03.960
We're going to come up here to our merged assets,

77
00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:07.159
and we will just go back over static mesh one

78
00:03:07.159 --> 00:03:08.199
and save that again.

79
00:03:08.199 --> 00:03:10.199
Yes, and that was pretty quick.

80
00:03:10.199 --> 00:03:13.319
So let's just inspect this and see what we have.

81
00:03:13.319 --> 00:03:15.400
So we have one mesh, there we go.

82
00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:18.080
One material, still 11,000 triangles.

83
00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:20.599
But again, that's not too big of an issue.

84
00:03:20.599 --> 00:03:24.120
Let's just pull this out and see what we have.

85
00:03:24.159 --> 00:03:27.039
Now one thing to notice, the statue

86
00:03:27.039 --> 00:03:32.599
itself is actually missing its kind of glass looking texture.

87
00:03:32.599 --> 00:03:34.590
This is one of the things that's kind

88
00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:35.710
of a trade-off between this tool.

89
00:03:35.710 --> 00:03:37.310
You have to make decisions about what

90
00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:39.030
you're going to merge together.

91
00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:41.909
I suggest that you merge things together

92
00:03:41.909 --> 00:03:43.870
that are of similar surface types.

93
00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:46.710
So all three of these would be good.

94
00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:48.469
The reason I would leave this alone

95
00:03:48.469 --> 00:03:51.990
is because of this unique glass material on it.

96
00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:54.870
Remember, merging actors is about optimization

97
00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:57.949
for performance, so we also don't

98
00:03:57.949 --> 00:03:59.389
want to destroy the artwork.

99
00:03:59.389 --> 00:04:01.430
So by merging all this down, we're destroying the artwork.

100
00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:03.030
So again, we're going to have to make

101
00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:04.509
trade-offs for different things.

102
00:04:04.509 --> 00:04:06.310
So let's delete this one out of the scene.

103
00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:09.789
We've still got a few other things in our merge actors

104
00:04:09.789 --> 00:04:10.430
that we can do.

105
00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:12.229
So we have looked at merge.

106
00:04:12.229 --> 00:04:14.349
Let's take a look at simplify.

107
00:04:14.349 --> 00:04:16.230
So let's just go ahead right away,

108
00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:18.310
and we'll leave this with the default settings,

109
00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:19.389
and we'll just merge.

110
00:04:19.389 --> 00:04:21.589
We're going to go up here to merged assets,

111
00:04:21.629 --> 00:04:25.069
and we will take this SMProxy chair and save over the top.

112
00:04:25.069 --> 00:04:27.230
Yes, and we'll give this just a moment to generate,

113
00:04:27.230 --> 00:04:31.560
and we'll be right back.

114
00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.920
All right, so that has finished generating.

115
00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:35.319
So let's go ahead and just take

116
00:04:35.319 --> 00:04:36.759
a look at what we got here.

117
00:04:36.759 --> 00:04:38.079
So here's our SMProxy chair.

118
00:04:38.079 --> 00:04:39.600
So let's open this up and see.

119
00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:40.720
We'll check that out.

120
00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:42.319
That's actually kind of cool.

121
00:04:42.319 --> 00:04:45.800
We're at 1,500 triangles, and we've

122
00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:47.639
got one mesh, one material.

123
00:04:47.639 --> 00:04:49.199
So what this has actually done is

124
00:04:49.199 --> 00:04:52.720
it's gone through and recreated this mesh for us.

125
00:04:52.759 --> 00:04:54.839
So let's just pull this out and have a look at it.

126
00:04:54.839 --> 00:04:56.759
So that's actually pretty good.

127
00:04:56.759 --> 00:04:59.040
That looks really, really good considering the fact

128
00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:01.040
that it did for us all automatically.

129
00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:02.279
So that is the simplify.

130
00:05:02.279 --> 00:05:04.439
Simplify is just going to take all that stuff,

131
00:05:04.439 --> 00:05:07.360
simplify it down, and give us a new mesh, new material.

132
00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:09.759
So it's going to reduce our poly and triangle count

133
00:05:09.759 --> 00:05:11.680
as well as reduce our material count.

134
00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:13.199
So that's a big win.

135
00:05:13.199 --> 00:05:15.399
So the next thing that we're going to look at here,

136
00:05:15.399 --> 00:05:18.399
I'm going to select these, and we're going to close this,

137
00:05:18.399 --> 00:05:20.399
and we're going to go to our batch.

138
00:05:20.439 --> 00:05:22.319
So what batch is going to do is it

139
00:05:22.319 --> 00:05:25.040
is going to take our static meshes

140
00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:29.000
and replace them with instanced static mesh versions.

141
00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:31.360
Now, one of the drawbacks to this

142
00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:34.519
is if we have multiple meshes selected

143
00:05:34.519 --> 00:05:36.600
that are all unique meshes, this tool's

144
00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:38.600
not going to work as well.

145
00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:40.759
If we notice right now, it says the current settings

146
00:05:40.759 --> 00:05:43.600
will result in one instanced static mesh component.

147
00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:45.160
Two actors will be replaced.

148
00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:46.759
And what that's telling me is basically

149
00:05:46.759 --> 00:05:49.639
it's only going to be able to replace the chairs because those

150
00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:51.839
are the only things that are identical.

151
00:05:51.839 --> 00:05:54.199
It can't replace the table or the statue

152
00:05:54.199 --> 00:05:57.000
because we don't have any other identical components

153
00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:57.720
for it.

154
00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:00.160
Now, I'm not going to merge the actors on this one,

155
00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:03.319
but I am going to kick it down to approximate.

156
00:06:03.319 --> 00:06:06.600
Now, what approximate does is it generates new mesh,

157
00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:08.079
new material, but this is more

158
00:06:08.079 --> 00:06:10.959
geared towards when you're trying to merge Nanite meshes.

159
00:06:10.959 --> 00:06:13.879
Now, it will work on regular static meshes,

160
00:06:13.879 --> 00:06:16.959
but it is geared towards Nanite meshes.

161
00:06:16.959 --> 00:06:18.920
Let's go ahead and see how this works by just

162
00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:21.480
clicking the Merge Actors button now.

163
00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:23.519
We're going to come up here to our merged assets,

164
00:06:23.519 --> 00:06:26.920
and we will do this approximate SM chair.

165
00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:30.040
Click Save, Override, and we'll be back in just a moment

166
00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:40.759
once this finishes.

167
00:06:40.759 --> 00:06:42.399
All right, now that that has finished,

168
00:06:42.399 --> 00:06:43.920
let's go ahead and see what the results are

169
00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:45.240
by hitting Control-Space.

170
00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:50.500
And I believe it was the right here.

171
00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:52.860
No, it was the approximate chair, so there we go.

172
00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:54.160
There's the approximate chair.

173
00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:56.939
So that has done quite a number on this.

174
00:06:56.939 --> 00:06:59.980
Let's go ahead and take a look at what its stats are.

175
00:06:59.980 --> 00:07:01.899
So 1998, that's pretty good.

176
00:07:01.899 --> 00:07:05.939
One mesh, one material, but we do have some issues there.

177
00:07:05.939 --> 00:07:09.220
We're getting some kind of crazy looking geometry.

178
00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:11.019
But again, remember, this is trying

179
00:07:11.019 --> 00:07:15.139
to work for Nanite meshes or meshes that have

180
00:07:15.139 --> 00:07:17.579
tons of detail inside of them.

181
00:07:17.579 --> 00:07:22.029
So when you are going through using Merge Actors,

182
00:07:22.029 --> 00:07:25.709
there are also a number of settings inside of this tool

183
00:07:25.709 --> 00:07:29.709
that will help adjust how everything is corrected.

184
00:07:29.709 --> 00:07:31.589
I highly suggest that everybody take a look

185
00:07:31.589 --> 00:07:35.339
at these settings and adjust, build,

186
00:07:35.339 --> 00:07:37.500
so you can more familiarize yourselves

187
00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:40.579
with how each mode in the Merge Actor tool

188
00:07:40.579 --> 00:07:46.500
works and how it can help benefit your project's meshes.
